            "count": 0,
            "text": "## Summary\n\n`model.matrix()` writes past a stack buffer when a column name fills its 4096-byte name buffer and the term has more variables, which aborts R 4.6.1 on macOS. Fixes a reproducible crash, and a column that took the previous column's name.\n\n## Details\n\n`modelmatrix()` in `src/library/stats/src/model.c` appends a name to the buffer only when it fits, warning otherwise, but appends the `:` between a term's variables unchecked: once a name is 4095 bytes long, each further variable writes one byte past the end. Separately, the buffer is not emptied between columns while the checks use `strlen(buf)`, so after a long name the next column's first name can be skipped, and the column keeps the previous name.\n\nReproducible example: attached as `model-matrix-colnames-buffer-repro.R`; in R 4.6.1, `~ A + B` with two 2100-byte names calls both columns `A`, and a child R running a 65-variable term aborts instead of printing a 4095-byte name.\n\nProposed patch: attached as `model-matrix-colnames-buffer.diff` (against r90968). It empties the buffer for each column and checks the separator like the names, with a test in `tests/reg-tests-1e.R`. `make check` passes, and the reproducer runs clean under ASan.\n\nThe patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/528, where CI results can be seen.\n\n## AI Disclosure\n\nThis patch was prepared with the assistance of Claude Code. The issue was discovered while investigating PR#19362.",
